WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.420
Cave-dwelling carnivores, parental possessions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:08.100
psychologists chased by psychopaths. It's&nbsp;
all been done before, and done again.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.680
Leave it to the film industry to release two&nbsp;
radically similar movies a month apart. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.200
M. Knight Shyamalan's film "The Sixth Sense" hit&nbsp;
big screens everywhere, it became a sensation. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.180
largely did so by positioning itself as a horror&nbsp;
film, then shifting gears and turning into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.920
thriller. The narrative twist is considered one of&nbsp;
M. Knight Shyamalan's best on-screen magic tricks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.240
and word of mouth made audiences feel like they&nbsp;
had to see it, putting the director on the map.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.320
However, many fans forget that another&nbsp;
film — one with a better pedigree — was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.620
released around the same time&nbsp;
and was devoured in its wake.

00:00:41.071 --> 00:00:43.291
"Could you just get to it, please?"

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.600
"Stir of Echoes" is based on Richard Matheson's&nbsp;
classic novel. The film adaptation was directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:53.340
by one of the most successful screenwriters of&nbsp;
all time, David Koepp, and stars Kevin Bacon.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.180
The film follows Tom, a working-class&nbsp;
man who lives in Chicago with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.140
pregnant wife. During a gathering one&nbsp;
evening, Tom allows his sister-in-law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:06.360
Lisa, to hypnotize him. While susceptible to&nbsp;
hypnotic suggestion, Lisa tells Tom to be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.560
open-minded. Afterward, he begins seeing&nbsp;
frightful visions of the dead. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.160
just like "The Sixth Sense," the&nbsp;
apparition isn't necessarily harmful.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.480
Both films feature lead characters who can&nbsp;
converse with the dead, ultimately helping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.860
the spirits resolve their worldly issues.&nbsp;
These similarities beg the question: why did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:28.380
"The Sixth Sense" gross about 32 times more at the&nbsp;
worldwide box office than its ghostly counterpart?

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.620
These two voyeuristic tales take people-watching&nbsp;
to the next level. In 1954, Alfred Hitchcock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:38.700
released his now-classic thriller, "Rear&nbsp;
Window," about a man recovering from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.200
broken leg in his apartment. To pass the time,&nbsp;
he begins watching his neighbors out of his rear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.180
window. When one neighbor's wife disappears,&nbsp;
the protagonist becomes increasingly convinced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.200
that he witnessed a murder, eventually roping&nbsp;
his girlfriend into the suspicion as well.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.880
53 years later, Shia LaBeouf would star&nbsp;
in "Disturbia," a film about a troubled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.900
teen reeling from the recent passing of&nbsp;
his father. Remanded to house arrest and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.160
similarly passing the time by people watching,&nbsp;
LaBeouf's character begins to feel there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:08.940
something off about the man next door. It's&nbsp;
a paranoid thriller for the new century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.600
and was one of the early films that&nbsp;
helped propel LaBeouf into stardom.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.020
"Disturbia" stirred some controversy in&nbsp;
the film industry when it was released.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.680
While "Rear Window" is based on&nbsp;
Cornell Woolrich's 1942 story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.260
"It Had To Be Murder," a 2008 lawsuit was filed&nbsp;
against Steven Spielberg and Dreamworks alleging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.980
that the studio had made "Disturbia" without&nbsp;
obtaining the rights to adapt the same story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:34.020
The claim was ultimately rejected, with the U.S.&nbsp;
District Court judge Laura Taylor Swan writing:

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.240
"The main plots are similar only at a high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.140
unprotectable level of generality [...]&nbsp;
Where 'Disturbia' is rife with subplots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.880
the short story has none. The setting and mood of&nbsp;
the short story are static and tense, whereas the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.380
setting and mood of 'Disturbia' are more dynamic&nbsp;
and peppered with humor and teen romance."

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.720
In the second half of the 20th century, the&nbsp;
idea of satanism began to take hold as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.800
threat to Americans, laying the groundwork&nbsp;
for the infamous Satanic Panic of the '80s.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.300
When Roman Polanski released "Rosemary's&nbsp;
Baby," it was a huge hit. The film starred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:08.040
Mia Farrow and John Cassavetes as a young couple&nbsp;
living in an upscale apartment in New York City.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.360
Featuring many cryptic, horrific moments&nbsp;
that ultimately lead to Rosemary becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.044
impregnated with the son of Satan, decades&nbsp;
later it still remains a discomforting watch.

00:03:17.044 --> 00:03:20.581
"Hail Satan! Satan is his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.581 --> 00:03:26.040
and his name is Adrian. He shall overthrow&nbsp;
the mighty and lay waste their temples."

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:30.960
In 1976, Richard Donner's "The Omen" was&nbsp;
dropped into the middle of this moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.580
with strong groundwork already laid for many&nbsp;
of its terrifying themes. Following Gregory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:39.720
Peck and Lee Remick as they experience&nbsp;
a deceptive childbirth that might just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:44.520
lead to them possessing the son of Satan, the&nbsp;
theme was unmistakably similar to "Rosemary's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.140
Baby." Both films essentially tell the same&nbsp;
story, ending on the same ominous idea:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:53.280
The antichrist has been born, and the&nbsp;
world is about to be plunged into darkness.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:58.620
John Krasinski blew audiences away&nbsp;
with 2018's "A Quiet Place," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:02.880
he wrote and directed. The film features&nbsp;
a post-apocalyptic world ravaged by blind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.380
otherworldly creatures hunting and killing&nbsp;
anything that makes a noise. Krasinski also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:11.880
starred in the film as a survivalist family&nbsp;
man guiding his family in the ways of this new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:16.560
reality. The family's daughter is deaf, making&nbsp;
her uniquely suited for humanity's response to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:20.640
their new predator. It was a pulse-pounding&nbsp;
movie, sure to give viewers a few gray hairs.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.194
[Man screams]

00:04:24.194 --> 00:04:28.260
In 2019, Netflix released the film&nbsp;
"The Silence," barely a year after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:32.700
Netflix's "Bird Box" had become a hit&nbsp;
as another sensory-driven horror film.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:36.840
"The Silence" features a family caught in&nbsp;
a post-apocalyptic world where a strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.760
species of creatures from underground have&nbsp;
emerged known as vesps. They viciously hunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.740
and kill based on sound and seek the areas with&nbsp;
the most noise for hunting. Protagonist Hugh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:51.480
played by Stanley Tucci, must work to ensure his&nbsp;
and his family's survival. Interestingly enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:55.080
Hugh's daughter, Ally, is also&nbsp;
deaf. In thrillers like these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.380
it seems, screenwriters are very&nbsp;
quietly stealing from each other.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:04.320
Ready for a claustrophobic adventure in the deep,&nbsp;
dark underground? If so, then these two films are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.640
for you. However, there's far more to be afraid of&nbsp;
than tight spaces and getting lost in the darkness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.800
in these subterranean thrillers. The 2005 Neil&nbsp;
Marshall breakout "The Descent" depicts a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.300
of thrill-seekers who decide to explore a strange&nbsp;
cave system. In an unsurprising turn of events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:22.680
they encounter predatory creatures long&nbsp;
trapped underground which hunt by using sound.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.480
2005's "The Cave" follows explorers who&nbsp;
attempt to investigate a cave system that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.560
has been covered for many years. Deep below the&nbsp;
surface, they soon discover that they've entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:36.360
the domain of horrific creatures never before&nbsp;
discovered by man who quickly develop a taste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:40.200
for the spelunkers. During their desperate&nbsp;
fight for survival, the remaining survivors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:45.000
learn that these creatures were once human, but&nbsp;
have been transformed by an unknown parasite.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.020
Which film pulled the concept off better? If&nbsp;
you aren't already feeling claustrophobic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.720
take a look for yourself. They'd&nbsp;
make a great double feature.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.640
Ed and Lorraine Warren became household&nbsp;
names with the cinematic depiction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:00.660
their paranormal exploits in "The Conjuring."&nbsp;
Reportedly, they were involved in a myriad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.740
of other supernatural investigations,&nbsp;
including the Enfield haunting and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:09.180
investigation into a certain haunted home in&nbsp;
Amityville, New York. That connection aside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:13.080
both "The Conjuring" and "The Amityville&nbsp;
Horror" have strikingly similar premises.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:18.480
2013's "The Conjuring" depicts the haunting&nbsp;
of the Perron family after they move into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.220
new home in Rhode Island. The home was once the&nbsp;
property of a witch by the name of Bathsheba,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:27.780
who killed her baby in the name of the devil&nbsp;
before cursing the plot of land and taking her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:32.580
life. The Warrens reveal that the spirit haunting&nbsp;
the Perron home is malevolent. It eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:36.600
attempts to possess the matriarch of the family&nbsp;
and force her to sacrifice her own child.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:41.160
"The Amityville Horror" also has a chilling&nbsp;
real-life backstory. The film depicts the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:45.780
Lutz family, who move into a home where&nbsp;
one year earlier Ronnie DeFeo murdered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:49.560
his entire family. Soon after moving&nbsp;
in, the family begins to experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:53.520
paranormal phenomena. The father,&nbsp;
George Lutz, played by James Brolin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.180
slowly becomes murderous, possessed&nbsp;
by the dark forces to harm his family.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.201
"I wouldn't hurt you."

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.140
Trauma often impacts people in different ways,&nbsp;
and can take the form of fixations or possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:09.000
agoraphobia. That's the case for Dr. Helen&nbsp;
Hudson in "Copycat," the would-be victim of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.740
ex-convict. Helen's criminal psychology expertise&nbsp;
allows her to better understand serial killers.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:17.880
She's then consulted by a homicide detective&nbsp;
to help solve a recent string of murders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:22.260
What she ultimately realizes is that&nbsp;
the murders are all copycat acts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:26.160
other notorious killers such as The&nbsp;
Hillside Strangler, Jeffrey Dahmer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:29.460
and Ted Bundy. Eventually the&nbsp;
murderer begins targeting Helen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.960
and everything the perpetrator does is connected&nbsp;
to a recent lecture she gave on serial killers.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:38.160
"Copycat" may not have been a hit, in fact&nbsp;
many have probably forgotten about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.920
but Netflix's 2021 film "The Woman In The Window"&nbsp;
has a lot in common with its 1995 predecessor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:48.360
The movie features a child psychologist named&nbsp;
Anna, played by Amy Adams, who is similarly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:53.220
agoraphobic due to past trauma and finds herself&nbsp;
observing her neighbors in her lonely state.

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:57.960
At one point, Anna witnesses the murder of a&nbsp;
woman on her street. After authorities tell her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:02.940
that nothing happened, she becomes a bit unsure&nbsp;
of her experiences. Later it is revealed that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.560
just like Dr. Hudson in "Copycat," a killer is&nbsp;
targeting Anna and has been toying with her.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:12.000
In this case, the similarities between the&nbsp;
two films are every bit as fishy as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.080
feel. "The Woman in the Window" is based&nbsp;
on a novel of the same name by A.J. Finn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.880
which is a pen name for Dan Mallory. He&nbsp;
endured a public relations nightmare after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.880
his book was published, and it was&nbsp;
revealed that he had been hiding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:27.840
fact that he had lifted plot points&nbsp;
from ... you guessed it, "Copycat."

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.820
While certainly not one of the most beloved M.&nbsp;
Night Shyamalan films, "The Happening" did cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:38.580
a stir among avid moviegoers drawn to its haunting&nbsp;
trailers. Starring Mark Wahlberg and Zooey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:43.260
Deschanel, the film depicts a couple attempting&nbsp;
to find safety from a strange plague that causes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:47.580
ordinary people to fatally harm themselves.&nbsp;
Beneath the violence and terror, the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:52.080
also sends a message about nature fighting back.&nbsp;
It's a strange film with an even stranger premise.

00:08:52.581 --> 00:08:53.350
"Oh No."

00:08:54.514 --> 00:08:55.415
"What, oh no?"

00:08:55.715 --> 00:08:59.820
"Bird Box" was released on Netflix in&nbsp;
2018, and it seemed to share a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:03.720
DNA with the Shyamalan film. "Bird Box"&nbsp;
follows Sandra Bullock and her children as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:07.920
they seek a safe haven from an apocalyptic&nbsp;
condition that has swept the entire globe.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.840
Something is causing people to take their own&nbsp;
lives, which occurs once they see "it." So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.360
in an effort to protect themselves,&nbsp;
survivors blind themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:20.000
employing bandanas or blindfolds to make sure&nbsp;
they never glimpse the evil creatures. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:25.020
harrowing film that promotes curiosity over its&nbsp;
biggest mysteries long after the credits roll.

